1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,994
Imagine stepping out of a car in the middle

2
00:00:02,994 --> 00:00:06,406
of the Sahara. In every direction, sand, rock and

3
00:00:06,406 --> 00:00:09,818
a burning sky. The nearest town might be hundreds

4
00:00:09,818 --> 00:00:13,229
of kilometers away. You have one bottle of water.

5
00:00:13,229 --> 00:00:16,432
How long would you last? Stay with me, because

6
00:00:16,432 --> 00:00:19,705
the answer, and the people and animals who live

7
00:00:19,705 --> 00:00:21,376
here, will surprise you.

8
00:00:21,926 --> 00:00:25,420
Welcome to Earth Decoded. If you love the most

9
00:00:25,420 --> 00:00:29,750
extreme places on our planet, tap subscribe. Today, we're

10
00:00:29,750 --> 00:00:33,169
walking into the biggest hot desert on Earth.

11
00:00:33,719 --> 00:00:37,868
The Sahara covers about nine million square kilometers of

12
00:00:37,868 --> 00:00:41,581
North Africa. That's roughly the size of the United

13
00:00:41,581 --> 00:00:45,367
States, or China, stretched across eleven countries.

14
00:00:45,917 --> 00:00:49,256
It's the largest hot desert on the planet. Only

15
00:00:49,256 --> 00:00:52,737
the cold deserts of Antarctica and the Arctic are

16
00:00:52,737 --> 00:00:53,234
bigger.

17
00:00:53,784 --> 00:00:57,897
And here's something most people don't know. Only about

18
00:00:57,897 --> 00:01:01,039
a quarter of the Sahara is sand dunes. The

19
00:01:01,039 --> 00:01:05,227
rest is bare rock plateaus, gravel plains, dry mountains

20
00:01:05,227 --> 00:01:06,648
and even volcanoes.

21
00:01:07,198 --> 00:01:10,769
The highest point is Emi Koussi, a huge volcano

22
00:01:10,769 --> 00:01:14,797
in Chad, more than three thousand four hundred meters

23
00:01:14,797 --> 00:01:15,177
tall.

24
00:01:15,727 --> 00:01:19,017
Where there are dunes, they can be giant. In

25
00:01:19,017 --> 00:01:22,906
places like Erg Chebbi in Morocco, dunes rise around

26
00:01:22,906 --> 00:01:26,645
one hundred fifty meters, as tall as a fifty-story

27
00:01:26,645 --> 00:01:29,487
building. And they move with the wind.

28
00:01:30,037 --> 00:01:33,450
Quick question. Would you rather explore the dunes or

29
00:01:33,450 --> 00:01:36,220
the mountains of the Sahara? Tell me in the

30
00:01:36,220 --> 00:01:36,799
comments.

31
00:01:37,349 --> 00:01:41,496
Now, the heat. In summer, afternoon air temperatures in

32
00:01:41,496 --> 00:01:46,171
parts of the Sahara regularly pass forty-five degrees Celsius.

33
00:01:46,171 --> 00:01:49,866
The sand itself can get far hotter, above seventy

34
00:01:49,866 --> 00:01:52,731
degrees, hot enough to burn your skin.

35
00:01:53,281 --> 00:01:56,068
There's almost no shade, and the sun is so

36
00:01:56,068 --> 00:01:59,519
strong that just standing still, you lose water fast

37
00:01:59,519 --> 00:02:00,449
through sweat.

38
00:02:00,999 --> 00:02:03,736
In that kind of heat, a person can need

39
00:02:03,736 --> 00:02:06,614
more than ten liters of water a day. Your

40
00:02:06,614 --> 00:02:10,334
one bottle wouldn't last a morning. Without water, in

41
00:02:10,334 --> 00:02:13,634
full sun, you could be in serious danger within

42
00:02:13,634 --> 00:02:14,055
a day.

43
00:02:14,605 --> 00:02:18,146
Most of the Sahara gets less than ten centimeters

44
00:02:18,146 --> 00:02:21,036
of rain a year. Some places can go years

45
00:02:21,036 --> 00:02:22,626
without a single drop.

46
00:02:23,176 --> 00:02:26,324
Why is it so dry? Warm, moist air rises

47
00:02:26,324 --> 00:02:30,361
near the equator, drops its rain over the tropics,

48
00:02:30,361 --> 00:02:34,074
then sinks again over North Africa as dry air.

49
00:02:34,074 --> 00:02:38,110
Sinking air blocks clouds from forming, so the sky

50
00:02:38,110 --> 00:02:40,371
stays clear almost all year.

51
00:02:40,921 --> 00:02:44,480
And the desert may be getting bigger. A 2018

52
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:48,121
study found the Sahara had grown by about ten

53
00:02:48,121 --> 00:02:52,490
percent since the 1920s, partly due to natural climate

54
00:02:52,490 --> 00:02:55,726
cycles and partly due to climate change.

55
00:02:56,276 --> 00:02:59,441
And then the sun goes down. With no clouds

56
00:02:59,441 --> 00:03:02,907
and dry air to hold the heat, temperatures can

57
00:03:02,907 --> 00:03:06,599
fall by more than twenty degrees. In winter, some

58
00:03:06,599 --> 00:03:09,161
desert nights drop below freezing.

59
00:03:09,711 --> 00:03:13,525
It has even snowed. In 2016 and again in

60
00:03:13,525 --> 00:03:18,197
2018, snow covered the orange dunes near the town

61
00:03:18,197 --> 00:03:20,485
of Aïn Sefra in Algeria.

62
00:03:21,035 --> 00:03:24,587
Then there are the sandstorms. Winds can lift walls

63
00:03:24,587 --> 00:03:27,722
of sand and dust hundreds of meters high that

64
00:03:27,722 --> 00:03:30,717
turn day into night. You can't see, you can

65
00:03:30,717 --> 00:03:33,643
barely breathe, and your tracks disappear.

66
00:03:34,193 --> 00:03:37,374
Even on calm days, the desert plays tricks on

67
00:03:37,374 --> 00:03:40,909
you. Hot air near the ground bends light, creating

68
00:03:40,909 --> 00:03:44,585
mirages that look like shining lakes on the horizon.

69
00:03:44,585 --> 00:03:48,403
Lost travelers have walked toward water that was never

70
00:03:48,403 --> 00:03:48,827
there.

71
00:03:49,377 --> 00:03:54,110
Saharan dust travels incredibly far. Satellites show it crossing

72
00:03:54,110 --> 00:03:57,438
the Atlantic every year, and some of it falls

73
00:03:57,438 --> 00:04:01,801
on the Amazon rainforest, where its minerals help fertilize

74
00:04:01,801 --> 00:04:02,540
the trees.

75
00:04:03,090 --> 00:04:05,922
Which would scare you more: the heat, the cold

76
00:04:05,922 --> 00:04:08,999
nights or the sandstorms? Tell me in the comments.

77
00:04:09,549 --> 00:04:13,833
In 1994, an Italian runner named Mauro Prosperi was

78
00:04:13,833 --> 00:04:18,117
racing in the Marathon des Sables, a multi-day race

79
00:04:18,117 --> 00:04:22,317
across the Sahara in Morocco. A sandstorm hit, and

80
00:04:22,317 --> 00:04:25,677
when it cleared, he was completely lost.

81
00:04:26,227 --> 00:04:29,474
He wandered for days. He sheltered in an old

82
00:04:29,474 --> 00:04:33,458
shrine, caught bats for food and drank whatever liquid

83
00:04:33,458 --> 00:04:37,220
he could. Finally, after about nine days, a nomadic

84
00:04:37,220 --> 00:04:41,204
family found him, more than two hundred kilometers off

85
00:04:41,204 --> 00:04:43,934
course, across the border in Algeria.

86
00:04:44,484 --> 00:04:47,347
He had lost more than a dozen kilos. His

87
00:04:47,347 --> 00:04:51,140
survival is considered almost a miracle, and it shows

88
00:04:51,140 --> 00:04:54,862
how quickly the Sahara can swallow someone who loses

89
00:04:54,862 --> 00:04:55,577
their way.

90
00:04:56,127 --> 00:04:59,998
Survival experts say that if your vehicle breaks down,

91
00:04:59,998 --> 00:05:03,080
the most important rule is: stay with it. A

92
00:05:03,080 --> 00:05:05,875
car is much easier to spot from the air

93
00:05:05,875 --> 00:05:09,028
than a person, and it gives shade. Walk only

94
00:05:09,028 --> 00:05:12,182
at night, and only if you're sure where help

95
00:05:12,182 --> 00:05:12,469
is.

96
00:05:13,019 --> 00:05:15,808
And yet, life finds a way. The camel can

97
00:05:15,808 --> 00:05:19,713
go days, even weeks in cooler weather, without drinking,

98
00:05:19,713 --> 00:05:22,921
and then drink more than one hundred liters in

99
00:05:22,921 --> 00:05:24,176
about ten minutes.

100
00:05:24,726 --> 00:05:28,023
The fennec fox, the smallest fox in the world,

101
00:05:28,023 --> 00:05:31,177
has giant ears that release heat and help it

102
00:05:31,177 --> 00:05:33,686
hear insects moving under the sand.

103
00:05:34,236 --> 00:05:37,219
And the Saharan silver ant comes out in the

104
00:05:37,219 --> 00:05:40,617
hottest part of the day, when its predators hide.

105
00:05:40,617 --> 00:05:44,848
Its silver hairs reflect sunlight, helping it survive surface

106
00:05:44,848 --> 00:05:47,761
temperatures that would kill most insects.

107
00:05:48,311 --> 00:05:51,729
People have lived here for thousands of years too.

108
00:05:51,729 --> 00:05:55,216
The Tuareg, often called the blue people because of

109
00:05:55,216 --> 00:05:59,454
their indigo-dyed clothing, have crossed the Sahara with camel

110
00:05:59,454 --> 00:06:01,026
caravans for centuries.

111
00:06:01,576 --> 00:06:05,955
For centuries, caravans carried slabs of salt from desert

112
00:06:05,955 --> 00:06:09,719
mines like Taoudenni in Mali south to cities like

113
00:06:09,719 --> 00:06:13,867
Timbuktu, trading them for gold, grain and cloth. Some

114
00:06:13,867 --> 00:06:17,171
salt caravans still make the journey today.

115
00:06:17,721 --> 00:06:21,273
Their secrets: travel in the cool hours, cover the

116
00:06:21,273 --> 00:06:24,470
body from the sun, know every well and oasis,

117
00:06:24,470 --> 00:06:26,105
and never travel alone.

118
00:06:26,655 --> 00:06:30,622
Oases are the lifelines of the desert, places where

119
00:06:30,622 --> 00:06:35,056
underground water reaches the surface. Towns like Siwa in

120
00:06:35,056 --> 00:06:39,412
Egypt have survived around oases for thousands of years.

121
00:06:39,962 --> 00:06:43,365
And from space, the Sahara has a giant eye.

122
00:06:43,365 --> 00:06:47,085
The Richat Structure in Mauritania is a ring of

123
00:06:47,085 --> 00:06:51,676
rock about forty kilometers wide, so large that astronauts

124
00:06:51,676 --> 00:06:53,338
use it as a landmark.

125
00:06:53,888 --> 00:06:56,995
Scientists think it formed as a dome of rock

126
00:06:56,995 --> 00:07:00,244
pushed up from below and then slowly worn away

127
00:07:00,244 --> 00:07:04,128
by wind and water, revealing rings of different layers.

128
00:07:04,678 --> 00:07:08,864
Remember, the Sahara wasn't always like this. Around eight

129
00:07:08,864 --> 00:07:12,618
thousand years ago, it was green, with lakes, hippos

130
00:07:12,618 --> 00:07:16,515
and grasslands. Rock art across the desert still shows

131
00:07:16,515 --> 00:07:17,670
that lost world.

132
00:07:18,220 --> 00:07:21,632
At the southern edge of the desert, Lake Chad

133
00:07:21,632 --> 00:07:25,422
shows how fast things can change. Since the 1960s,

134
00:07:25,422 --> 00:07:29,062
it has lost around ninety percent of its surface

135
00:07:29,062 --> 00:07:33,004
area, affecting millions of people who depend on it.

136
00:07:33,554 --> 00:07:37,004
And the Sahara could power the future. It receives

137
00:07:37,004 --> 00:07:40,868
so much sunshine that scientists have calculated a small

138
00:07:40,868 --> 00:07:43,904
part of it covered in solar panels could, in

139
00:07:43,904 --> 00:07:47,975
theory, produce more electricity than the whole world uses.

140
00:07:48,525 --> 00:07:51,796
So, could you survive the Sahara alone? With one

141
00:07:51,796 --> 00:07:55,067
bottle of water, probably not for long. But with

142
00:07:55,067 --> 00:07:58,610
the knowledge of the people who live here, traveling

143
00:07:58,610 --> 00:08:01,676
at the right times, from well to well, humans

144
00:08:01,676 --> 00:08:04,333
have crossed it for thousands of years.

145
00:08:04,883 --> 00:08:08,852
Last question. Which extreme place should we explore next?

146
00:08:08,852 --> 00:08:10,494
Tell me in the comments.

147
00:08:11,044 --> 00:08:14,796
Thanks for watching Earth Decoded. If you enjoyed this,

148
00:08:14,796 --> 00:08:17,933
like the video, subscribe, and share it with a

149
00:08:17,933 --> 00:08:21,139
friend who loves adventure. See you in the next

150
00:08:21,139 --> 00:08:21,412
one.

